<h2>The case the Family Court accepted</h2>
<p>The parties married in 2006 and have two children. The husband's petition under Section 13(1)(i-a) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 painted a grim picture: refusal of conjugal relations, separate living within the same railway quarters, threats, administration of poison, boiling water poured on him, an attempt to set him alight, abusive messages, and a design on his railway job. He pointed to a criminal case against the wife and other proceedings as confirmation. The Family Court at Dhanbad accepted the case in June 2024, dissolved the marriage, and awarded the wife Rs. 12 lakh as permanent alimony.</p>
<p>The wife appealed against the decree; the husband cross-appealed against the alimony alone. The Division Bench reserved judgment at the end of August and pronounced on 30 October 2026.</p>

<h2>Why the decree could not stand</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Complaints treated as proof.</strong> The Family Court had leaned on the existence of criminal proceedings between the spouses. The High Court held that institution of a case establishes nothing about the truth of its contents.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Assertion without corroboration.</strong> The husband's testimony on the refusal of relations and the violent incidents stood uncorroborated; for allegations of poisoning and burning, no medical or contemporaneous record supported the account.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The daughter's evidence.</strong> Considered, but found an unsafe foundation for dissolving the marriage absent independent support.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct cutting the other way.</strong> The husband had entered the wife's name in his service records and obtained a railway pass for her — conduct difficult to reconcile with the marriage he described. He had also never sought restitution of conjugal rights.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The Bench was careful to ring-fence the criminal docket: its judgment records no finding of guilt or innocence in any pending criminal case. The matrimonial record simply failed to prove cruelty, so the decree fell — and with it, the alimony order that presupposed a divorce.</p>
</div>

<h2>The standard this judgment enforces</h2>
<p>Matrimonial courts operate on the civil standard — preponderance of probabilities — but preponderance still requires probative material. Separate living under one roof, the court noted, does not by itself establish cruelty; marriages subsist through estrangements. Nor can the matrimonial forum outsource its fact-finding to the criminal process, in either direction: acquittal does not disprove cruelty, pendency does not prove it, and a decree that rests on the existence of litigation rather than the proof of conduct is built on sand.</p>
<p>The decision pairs instructively with the same Bench's rulings delivered a day earlier, dismissing husbands' appeals where cruelty and desertion were unproved. Together they describe a consistent discipline: whoever seeks the decree proves the ground; whoever resists it is entitled to see the evidence tested, not assumed.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Grave allegations need grave proof: medical records for injuries, promptly lodged complaints, witnesses with direct knowledge. Build the record contemporaneously, not at trial.</p>
<p>Inventory your own conduct — service records, nominations, passes, remittances — because the other side will read it as an admission of a functioning marriage.</p>
<p>A respondent facing a decree granted on thin evidence should consider a first appeal under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act within the limitation period; appellate benches do re-weigh the record.</p>
<p>Where divorce is set aside, dependent orders — alimony, and arrangements premised on dissolution — fall with it; plan the appellate strategy as a whole.</p>
</div>
